  <section class="about fade-up d2">
    <span class="hanko">Colophon</span>
    <h1>Janmejai Singh <span class="hl">Minhas</span></h1>

    <div class="body">
      <p>
        I build machine learning systems that have to work outside a notebook. Most of my
        work has lived in computer vision — medical imaging, mostly — where the gap between
        a good validation score and something a clinician would actually trust is where all
        the real engineering happens.
      </p>

      <p>
        That gap is what this journal is about. Benchmarks are easy to read and easy to
        overfit to. What is harder, and more useful, is writing down what breaks when a
        model meets data it was not curated for, what the research actually claims once you
        read past the abstract, and what a deployment costs when you account for the parts
        nobody benchmarks.
      </p>

      <h2>Research</h2>

      <p>
        Two peer-reviewed papers, both from work on diagnostic imaging:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fused framework for glaucoma diagnosis using Optical Coherence Tomography
          images</strong> — <em>Expert Systems with Applications</em> (Elsevier).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Denoising techniques for cephalometric X-ray images: a comprehensive
          review</strong> — <em>Multimedia Tools and Applications</em> (Springer).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Both came out of the Design Innovation Centre (MDaRT) at Panjab University, where I
        worked on computer-aided diagnosis — glaucoma detection from OCT scans, pancreatic
        cancer identification from CT, and COVID classification from chest X-rays. I later
        led the AI and web teams there, running a group of about twenty interns and teaching
        deep learning workshops that put roughly sixty students each through a summer
        program.
      </p>

      <h2>Engineering</h2>

      <p>
        At <strong>MARG ERP</strong> in New Delhi I worked as a Python developer, building
        OCR-based receipt processing that cut manual data-entry time by around 80% and
        automating the spreadsheet pipelines finance teams were maintaining by hand.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before that, a remote research fellowship with <strong>Nottingham Trent
        University</strong> on heat-transfer optimisation for lithium-ion battery packs —
        sensor-data analysis in Python to map thermal management arrangements. Different
        domain, same underlying problem: making measurements say something reliable.
      </p>

